1
00:00:09,720 --> 00:00:12,640
*loud ticking*
*suspenseful music*

2
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:22,440
Hey, you there!

3
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:26,320
Where are you going?

4
00:00:27,320 --> 00:00:28,520
Inside.

5
00:00:28,680 --> 00:00:29,880
Come here.

6
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:43,080
Oh, SA.

7
00:00:44,760 --> 00:00:47,200
What do you want here?
- Making a delivery.

8
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:48,280
Not tonight.

9
00:00:48,440 --> 00:00:50,400
It's closed, this joint.
Closing time.

10
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:06,640
*music builds gradually*

11
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:34,800
We need to get over there.

12
00:01:44,120 --> 00:01:45,200
This is where it has to go.

13
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:05,200
*music continues*

14
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:14,360
*rapid clock ticking*

15
00:02:25,720 --> 00:02:28,360
(Doorman) Mr. Jacoby. Good evening.

16
00:02:47,760 --> 00:02:49,800
*rapid clock ticking*

17
00:03:05,880 --> 00:03:06,880
*Jacoby sighs*

18
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:18,440
*quiet murmur of conversation*

19
00:03:19,320 --> 00:03:20,640
*he whispers indistinctly*

20
00:03:46,440 --> 00:03:48,560
Mr. Nyssen, we can begin.

21
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:53,160
Yes, let our guest of honor
have another sip first.

22
00:03:55,320 --> 00:03:57,160
*music becomes more ominous*

23
00:03:57,240 --> 00:03:59,040
But he doesn't drink alcohol.

24
00:03:59,120 --> 00:04:02,520
Exactly.
Every three minutes he has to refuse.

25
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:04,840
(Helga) Alfred, please.

26
00:04:06,120 --> 00:04:08,480
This pompous ass of a...
- No, thank you.

27
00:04:10,680 --> 00:04:12,800
Ma'am,
there's no fizz left at all.

28
00:04:12,880 --> 00:04:14,920
I'd be happy
to bring you a fresh one.

29
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:29,160
(Helga) Please, restrain yourself.

30
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:31,560
(whispers) Trust me.

31
00:04:33,320 --> 00:04:34,800
(Göring) You're quite right.

32
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:36,920
*tense music*

33
00:04:37,920 --> 00:04:41,040
Long live our new
Reich Chancellor, Adolf Hitler!

34
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:42,640
Three cheers!

35
00:04:42,720 --> 00:04:44,600
(All) Hurrah! Hurrah!

36
00:04:44,760 --> 00:04:46,960
*clinking of glasses*

37
00:04:47,760 --> 00:04:49,440
*engine sputters*

38
00:04:53,720 --> 00:04:54,880
*he groans*

39
00:04:56,840 --> 00:04:57,840
Charlotte!

40
00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:01,200
There.

41
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:04,240
*glass shatters*

42
00:05:27,560 --> 00:05:28,760
(Gräf) Guard!

43
00:05:29,440 --> 00:05:31,960
Criminal Police.
Someone climbed in over there.

44
00:05:32,040 --> 00:05:34,000
Where?
- Up there!

45
00:05:35,080 --> 00:05:36,240
(Gräf) Oh, God.

46
00:05:37,280 --> 00:05:40,840
Alert the fire station
on Unter den Linden. Hurry! Come on!

47
00:05:45,200 --> 00:05:46,720
*loud whistles*

48
00:05:46,800 --> 00:05:48,320
*fire crackles*

49
00:05:50,880 --> 00:05:53,920
*quiet conversations*

50
00:05:56,880 --> 00:05:59,000
*conversations fall silent*

51
00:06:02,840 --> 00:06:04,600
*water gurgling*

52
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:31,720
(whispers) Ten more minutes.

53
00:06:39,640 --> 00:06:42,560
Beloved Helga, honored guests,

54
00:06:43,080 --> 00:06:46,840
and last but not least,
dear Mr. Hitler!

55
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:49,600
It has been nearly
a full month now

56
00:06:49,680 --> 00:06:54,120
since we appointed you
chairman of German Reich, Inc.

57
00:06:54,200 --> 00:06:57,840
If I may introduce,
from left to right,

58
00:06:58,360 --> 00:07:00,560
Günther Quandt, Friedrich Flick,

59
00:07:00,640 --> 00:07:03,240
Carl Bosch, Alfried Krupp,

60
00:07:03,320 --> 00:07:06,280
Ferdinand Porsche, Karl Merck,

61
00:07:06,360 --> 00:07:08,720
Wilhelm von Opel,
Hermann von Siemens,

62
00:07:08,800 --> 00:07:11,240
Franz von Papen
and Alfred Hugenberg.

63
00:07:11,320 --> 00:07:14,880
Yes, I know all of you,
gentlemen.

64
00:07:15,680 --> 00:07:18,040
I didn't think I had to introduce
the board of directors

65
00:07:18,120 --> 00:07:20,720
of this corporation to you,

66
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:23,680
but does the dog remember
the hand that feeds it?

67
00:07:23,760 --> 00:07:25,560
*indignant murmuring*
*Helga laughs*

68
00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:29,480
(Hitler)
What did you just say, Nyssen?

69
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:31,960
Yes, what did I say?

70
00:07:32,040 --> 00:07:33,960
I'm referring
to my ten-point plan,

71
00:07:34,040 --> 00:07:36,560
which, in support
of your campaign,

72
00:07:36,720 --> 00:07:38,600
I had delivered to you.

73
00:07:39,080 --> 00:07:41,720
Point one: economic policy.

74
00:07:42,280 --> 00:07:45,240
It shall and will
be guided by the state.

75
00:07:45,320 --> 00:07:47,600
The state dictates
what the nation needs,

76
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:50,840
and industry —
all of us here — will deliver.

77
00:07:50,920 --> 00:07:53,520
You will find that it isn't
entirely wrong...

78
00:07:53,600 --> 00:07:54,720
*she retches*

79
00:07:55,960 --> 00:07:57,200
*Helga groans*

80
00:07:57,360 --> 00:07:59,120
*low, menacing music*

81
00:07:59,280 --> 00:08:00,640
(whispers) Mrs. Nyssen.

82
00:08:02,840 --> 00:08:04,200
Helga?

83
00:08:04,360 --> 00:08:06,000
*quiet murmuring*

84
00:08:06,520 --> 00:08:09,280
Come with me,
I'll escort you outside.

85
00:08:10,640 --> 00:08:11,760
Helga?

86
00:08:13,560 --> 00:08:15,080
*Helga groans*

87
00:08:20,240 --> 00:08:22,360
I'm sure she'll feel better
in just a moment.

88
00:08:22,440 --> 00:08:27,560
Where was I?
Right. Economic policy.

89
00:08:28,480 --> 00:08:31,000
It shall be national, but...

90
00:08:31,080 --> 00:08:32,840
(Wegener) Please come with me.

91
00:08:33,600 --> 00:08:34,799
Wegener, what is this?

92
00:08:36,200 --> 00:08:39,000
Pardon me, ma'am,
but we need to go this way.

93
00:08:39,159 --> 00:08:40,159
Where to?
- This way.

94
00:08:40,240 --> 00:08:42,200
(Edgar) Where are you taking her?
- Out.

95
00:08:42,919 --> 00:08:44,159
*Helga continues retching*

96
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:02,040
That isn't entirely wrong.

97
00:09:02,200 --> 00:09:04,080
*music becomes more dramatic*

98
00:09:07,560 --> 00:09:09,000
*he sighs*

99
00:09:15,360 --> 00:09:18,600
What is it? What are you waiting for?
- The connection is down.

100
00:09:19,080 --> 00:09:20,160
What?

101
00:09:25,160 --> 00:09:26,280
Damn it!

102
00:09:35,240 --> 00:09:36,840
The timed detonator?

103
00:09:38,040 --> 00:09:39,160
*he sighs*

104
00:09:42,920 --> 00:09:44,040
Let's go.

105
00:09:45,640 --> 00:09:47,640
*Helga groans in pain*

106
00:09:50,520 --> 00:09:53,320
(Wegener, whispering) What happened?
- Gereon?

107
00:09:53,800 --> 00:09:55,080
We have to go back in.

108
00:09:55,160 --> 00:09:56,360
What?
- Yes.

109
00:09:56,520 --> 00:09:58,280
Gereon, what are you doing here?

110
00:10:00,280 --> 00:10:01,760
What are you up to?

111
00:10:02,280 --> 00:10:04,120
Mrs. Nyssen...
- Let me go!

112
00:10:04,200 --> 00:10:06,160
Ma'am, you must...
- No!

113
00:10:06,240 --> 00:10:08,280
Mrs. Nyssen!
- No! No, Gereon! No!

114
00:10:08,360 --> 00:10:10,720
Alfred! No! Let me go!
- That's enough, Helga!

115
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:13,240
Alfred! No! Alfred!
- Please come with me.

116
00:10:13,320 --> 00:10:14,400
Alfred!

117
00:10:15,960 --> 00:10:18,800
You, your jacket. Quickly!
- Gentlemen, the white wine.

118
00:10:19,760 --> 00:10:22,200
Point six, one of the most important.

119
00:10:22,360 --> 00:10:24,440
And if you'll permit me to note,

120
00:10:24,600 --> 00:10:28,640
one of the points closest to my heart
personally:

121
00:10:29,400 --> 00:10:30,800
autarky.

122
00:10:32,240 --> 00:10:34,480
One moment, please.
I'm not finished yet.

123
00:10:38,360 --> 00:10:41,720
We consider it vital that
the sustenance of our people

124
00:10:41,800 --> 00:10:44,840
is secured
within our own borders.

125
00:10:45,280 --> 00:10:48,680
Well, now it actually fits
quite nicely, doesn't it?

126
00:10:49,520 --> 00:10:50,760
Let us now turn to the subject

127
00:10:50,920 --> 00:10:55,400
that presumably interests you
all the most: weapons.

128
00:10:56,840 --> 00:10:58,480
Watch out!
- Pardon me.

129
00:10:59,080 --> 00:11:00,160
Oops.

130
00:11:01,200 --> 00:11:02,680
(Guest) My pants are wet.

131
00:11:02,840 --> 00:11:06,800
As you know, my engineers
have been developing

132
00:11:06,960 --> 00:11:08,640
various types of rockets
for quite some time.

133
00:11:09,080 --> 00:11:13,480
Especially when it comes
to colonization,

134
00:11:13,560 --> 00:11:16,200
we should harness this technology.

135
00:11:16,360 --> 00:11:19,440
Consider this: from outer space,

136
00:11:19,520 --> 00:11:22,360
we can reach
any point on Earth

137
00:11:22,440 --> 00:11:24,320
in a matter of seconds.

138
00:11:24,480 --> 00:11:27,920
Any point,
any country, any continent.

139
00:11:28,680 --> 00:11:31,240
The union
of military weaponry

140
00:11:31,320 --> 00:11:33,000
and technological progress

141
00:11:33,160 --> 00:11:35,560
will, in the shortest possible time,

142
00:11:35,640 --> 00:11:38,400
propel Germany
to the top of the world.

143
00:11:38,560 --> 00:11:43,160
The Nyssen Corporation stands ready,
ready, anytime and anywhere,

144
00:11:43,320 --> 00:11:45,720
to unleash the elemental forces
for the subjugation of the enemy

145
00:11:45,800 --> 00:11:47,840
and of gravity itself!

146
00:11:56,240 --> 00:11:57,920
*eerie music*

147
00:12:23,280 --> 00:12:24,920
*rhythmic music*

148
00:12:41,440 --> 00:12:43,320
*fire bell rings*

149
00:12:44,640 --> 00:12:46,520
*chaotic clamor of voices*

150
00:12:56,720 --> 00:12:58,520
Hurry up, let's get out of here!

151
00:13:01,880 --> 00:13:03,800
You can't go any farther.
It's on fire.

152
00:13:03,960 --> 00:13:06,400
We have to get through.
- Not now.

153
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:08,760
This could blow at any second.

154
00:13:11,440 --> 00:13:14,440
Who are they?
What's in those canisters?

155
00:13:15,240 --> 00:13:16,680
*he hisses*

156
00:13:18,080 --> 00:13:19,080
Get lost!

157
00:13:23,760 --> 00:13:25,280
That wasn't the fire department.

158
00:13:26,800 --> 00:13:29,400
You go up top,
I'll take this floor, all right?

159
00:13:29,560 --> 00:13:30,600
Yes.

160
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:33,680
...this will be the Germany

161
00:13:33,760 --> 00:13:36,400
that we, in this circle,
have newly envisioned,

162
00:13:36,480 --> 00:13:37,720
made reality.

163
00:13:38,400 --> 00:13:41,080
Minister of Economy
will be Alfred Krupp,

164
00:13:41,720 --> 00:13:45,400
though the Defense Ministry
is probably in the best hands

165
00:13:45,480 --> 00:13:47,080
with me,

166
00:13:47,160 --> 00:13:51,320
as I was already able to lay out
for you under point six.

167
00:13:52,560 --> 00:13:54,200
*Wendt whispers indistinctly*

168
00:13:54,840 --> 00:13:57,280
Yes, that brings me directly to...

169
00:13:57,920 --> 00:13:59,600
to point nine.

170
00:13:59,760 --> 00:14:02,360
I beg your pardon,
but we are confronted here

171
00:14:02,440 --> 00:14:05,400
with an extraordinary situation.

172
00:14:05,480 --> 00:14:07,520
The Reich Chancellor
and Reichstag President

173
00:14:07,600 --> 00:14:09,840
are urgently needed in the city.

174
00:14:09,920 --> 00:14:11,360
*confused murmuring*

175
00:14:15,560 --> 00:14:18,200
Perhaps we should
continue our consultation

176
00:14:18,360 --> 00:14:20,280
at another time.

177
00:14:20,440 --> 00:14:22,200
Out!
*murmuring stops*

178
00:14:25,600 --> 00:14:26,600
Everyone out!

179
00:14:27,720 --> 00:14:30,520
Out!
- Outrageous!

180
00:14:31,840 --> 00:14:33,520
*indignant clamor of voices*

181
00:14:50,520 --> 00:14:52,400
*quiet ticking*

182
00:14:57,760 --> 00:15:00,600
(Annemarie)
What a pathetic worm.

183
00:15:00,680 --> 00:15:02,200
*ominous music*

184
00:15:02,280 --> 00:15:06,560
The mightier your will to grandeur,
the more obvious

185
00:15:06,640 --> 00:15:09,840
your destiny
to total irrelevance.

186
00:15:10,520 --> 00:15:14,520
You are nothing,
an infantile wannabe,

187
00:15:14,680 --> 00:15:16,760
a pitiful nobody.
- Silence, Mother.

188
00:15:16,840 --> 00:15:18,520
You will not shut me up.

189
00:15:18,680 --> 00:15:21,000
Shh!
What is this?

190
00:15:21,080 --> 00:15:23,400
*rapid ticking, dripping*

191
00:15:26,160 --> 00:15:27,240
Do you hear that?

192
00:15:28,560 --> 00:15:29,680
Something's ticking.

193
00:15:30,400 --> 00:15:33,120
Nonsense, you're the one ticking.

194
00:15:33,280 --> 00:15:34,640
And not quite right, at that.

195
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:39,200
Something's there.

196
00:15:46,960 --> 00:15:48,240
That is a bomb.

197
00:15:49,120 --> 00:15:51,480
An explosive charge, no doubt.

198
00:15:51,560 --> 00:15:54,280
You have to defuse it, boy!

199
00:15:55,320 --> 00:15:57,520
In less than a minute,
the charge goes off.

200
00:15:57,680 --> 00:15:59,760
We have to get out of here! Move!

201
00:16:00,480 --> 00:16:01,640
You're coming with me!

202
00:16:02,600 --> 00:16:03,600
No.

203
00:16:03,680 --> 00:16:05,800
You'll do as I say!
- No!

204
00:16:05,960 --> 00:16:07,920
*somber music*

205
00:16:11,480 --> 00:16:12,520
Go on, then.

206
00:16:19,200 --> 00:16:20,920
(sobbing) Come with me.

207
00:16:22,400 --> 00:16:23,880
What?
- Come with me.

208
00:16:25,280 --> 00:16:27,120
I'm begging you.

209
00:16:29,080 --> 00:16:30,200
You're begging me?

210
00:16:30,280 --> 00:16:32,480
Yes, with all my heart.

211
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:34,840
Leave this room.

212
00:16:36,720 --> 00:16:38,680
*music grows more somber*

213
00:16:38,760 --> 00:16:41,840
You want to keep me in this world
as long as you can,

214
00:16:42,720 --> 00:16:44,960
so you won't have to face me
in the next.

215
00:16:45,960 --> 00:16:47,360
I'm imploring you.

216
00:16:49,200 --> 00:16:51,240
My dear boy,

217
00:16:51,400 --> 00:16:56,920
my dear,
my dear, dear boy, I...

218
00:16:57,920 --> 00:17:01,560
I love you so very much.

219
00:17:03,320 --> 00:17:04,520
Please.

220
00:17:09,839 --> 00:17:11,040
*he sniffles*

221
00:17:15,920 --> 00:17:17,359
It's all right, Mother.

222
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:20,319
We'll go together.

223
00:17:22,200 --> 00:17:23,720
Both of us.

224
00:17:27,720 --> 00:17:29,320
*glass shatters*

225
00:17:36,160 --> 00:17:37,800
*Edgar gasps for breath*

226
00:17:51,560 --> 00:17:53,400
*tense, pulsing music*

227
00:18:15,960 --> 00:18:17,800
*upbeat music playing*

228
00:18:21,600 --> 00:18:24,640
Jacoby? Forget something?

229
00:18:47,800 --> 00:18:49,560
*door handle rattles*

230
00:18:49,720 --> 00:18:52,480
What is this?
Open the door!

231
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:02,920
*crackling*

232
00:19:05,120 --> 00:19:07,040
*muffled rumbling*

233
00:19:12,360 --> 00:19:13,600
Hands up!

234
00:19:18,600 --> 00:19:19,720
Freeze!

235
00:19:26,960 --> 00:19:28,560
*fire bell rings*

236
00:19:31,480 --> 00:19:34,200
*agitated clamor of voices*

237
00:19:46,120 --> 00:19:49,440
There will be consequences.
You are aware of that, yes?

238
00:19:50,760 --> 00:19:52,920
(Harry) Mr. Jacoby.
- Przybylski!

239
00:19:53,080 --> 00:19:54,480
Open the door!

240
00:19:54,640 --> 00:19:56,520
What are you still doing here?
- Przybylski!

241
00:19:57,440 --> 00:19:59,160
I said open the door!

242
00:20:01,120 --> 00:20:02,320
Do you trust me?

243
00:20:04,600 --> 00:20:05,600
Yes.

244
00:20:08,200 --> 00:20:09,880
What are you planning?

245
00:20:12,680 --> 00:20:14,080
*music cuts off*

246
00:20:17,640 --> 00:20:19,080
*radio static*

247
00:20:30,160 --> 00:20:32,880
Citizens of the German Reich.

248
00:20:36,040 --> 00:20:39,400
In a few days, you will elect
a new parliament.

249
00:20:40,120 --> 00:20:44,680
Who you give your vote to
is your decision.

250
00:20:47,680 --> 00:20:52,720
The people are the sovereign
of a nation, and no one else.

251
00:20:53,560 --> 00:20:58,640
Any political force
that opposes this principle

252
00:20:58,720 --> 00:21:00,600
must be stopped.

253
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:06,000
Today, we have stood up against

254
00:21:06,640 --> 00:21:11,760
the false path
of a Führer principle.

255
00:21:14,840 --> 00:21:18,400
The Reich Chancellor
Adolf Hitler is dead.

256
00:21:21,280 --> 00:21:26,240
He and his followers
forced us to this act of violence.

257
00:21:27,040 --> 00:21:32,400
For the alliance of fascism
and capital has only one goal.

258
00:21:33,680 --> 00:21:34,960
War.

259
00:21:36,560 --> 00:21:41,720
In war, a person loses
his soul, his compassion.

260
00:21:44,360 --> 00:21:46,640
His love of nature and the world.

261
00:21:48,680 --> 00:21:52,960
What remains is a specter,

262
00:21:54,680 --> 00:21:57,360
shattered in body and mind.

263
00:21:59,760 --> 00:22:04,520
We,
we ourselves are these specters.

264
00:22:04,600 --> 00:22:05,840
We,

265
00:22:06,480 --> 00:22:10,800
the former fellow soldiers,
the ghosts of war.

266
00:22:10,960 --> 00:22:13,080
*mournful violin music*

267
00:22:13,160 --> 00:22:17,600
Today, we have killed
one of our own.

268
00:22:19,040 --> 00:22:23,640
It was our final duty
to this country.

269
00:22:26,120 --> 00:22:30,280
Even a hundred years from now,
people will marvel

270
00:22:30,360 --> 00:22:34,720
at what we accomplished
in those few years after the war.

271
00:22:34,880 --> 00:22:38,280
The achievements
of science and medicine,

272
00:22:38,360 --> 00:22:43,200
of psychology and pharmacology,
of film and literature,

273
00:22:43,280 --> 00:22:47,480
the rights of women,
free art and a free press,

274
00:22:47,640 --> 00:22:50,040
free speech
and constructive debate,

275
00:22:50,120 --> 00:22:52,120
and not least of all,

276
00:22:52,200 --> 00:22:55,960
the unwavering stance
against war.

277
00:22:56,840 --> 00:23:01,560
Hatred, envy, violence,

278
00:23:02,400 --> 00:23:04,520
all of this arises only

279
00:23:04,600 --> 00:23:09,120
from the oppressive nature
of authoritarianism.

280
00:23:13,120 --> 00:23:15,800
*clamor of voices*

281
00:23:26,680 --> 00:23:29,000
*mournful piano music begins*

282
00:23:32,200 --> 00:23:34,120
*he breathes in a panic*

283
00:23:39,360 --> 00:23:40,680
(Anno) Our deed

284
00:23:41,240 --> 00:23:44,320
will prevent the German people
from degenerating

285
00:23:44,400 --> 00:23:49,680
into human machines,
without feeling, without mercy,

286
00:23:49,760 --> 00:23:51,520
and without love.

287
00:23:52,360 --> 00:23:54,080
*tires screech*

288
00:24:00,760 --> 00:24:03,400
We are not machines.

289
00:24:03,560 --> 00:24:08,400
We are only the broken automata
of the last war,

290
00:24:08,480 --> 00:24:13,440
and we now step back
into the darkness.

291
00:24:13,600 --> 00:24:15,920
Open up!
Unlock the door!

292
00:24:16,080 --> 00:24:17,640
*door handle rattles*

293
00:24:17,720 --> 00:24:19,200
What is going on here?

294
00:24:19,280 --> 00:24:20,360
*sounds of struggle*

295
00:24:28,920 --> 00:24:32,400
(Anno) But with this deed,
we have made room

296
00:24:34,120 --> 00:24:36,800
for the new and the free
in Germany,

297
00:24:38,040 --> 00:24:40,360
for the open and the vast
in the world.

298
00:24:43,920 --> 00:24:47,880
It is now up to you,
the German people,

299
00:24:48,520 --> 00:24:51,080
to chart the path ahead.

300
00:24:56,920 --> 00:24:58,240
Choose it...

301
00:24:58,400 --> 00:24:59,920
*radio static*

302
00:25:02,800 --> 00:25:04,680
In the name of humanity.

303
00:25:08,960 --> 00:25:10,480
Potocznik, do something.

304
00:25:11,440 --> 00:25:13,160
In the name of dignity

305
00:25:14,400 --> 00:25:17,760
and in the name of freedom.

306
00:25:26,480 --> 00:25:28,200
*van der Lubbe breathes shakily*

307
00:25:38,320 --> 00:25:39,600
(van der Lubbe) Get out of here!

308
00:25:41,080 --> 00:25:42,600
I'll end this!

309
00:25:45,240 --> 00:25:47,160
Police! Drop it, now!

310
00:25:50,800 --> 00:25:52,040
Put it out, damn it!

311
00:25:59,120 --> 00:26:00,800
*draft of air*

312
00:26:00,960 --> 00:26:03,560
*rustling, crackling*

313
00:26:07,720 --> 00:26:09,400
*dark, dramatic music*

314
00:26:19,560 --> 00:26:21,080
*Gräf coughs*

315
00:26:26,440 --> 00:26:28,400
(Firefighter)
Everyone out, immediately!

316
00:26:32,560 --> 00:26:34,240
There, got you!
- Charlotte!

317
00:26:38,760 --> 00:26:40,520
*van der Lubbe groans*

318
00:26:40,680 --> 00:26:42,760
Come on! Come on, get out!
- Go, move!

319
00:26:48,640 --> 00:26:50,360
*muffled bang*

320
00:26:55,760 --> 00:26:57,480
*startled cries*

321
00:26:57,960 --> 00:26:59,360
*she coughs*

322
00:27:06,200 --> 00:27:08,560
Gereon, stay here!

323
00:27:08,720 --> 00:27:09,880
Let me go!

324
00:27:15,560 --> 00:27:16,600
*he coughs*

325
00:27:16,760 --> 00:27:18,000
Get out of here!

326
00:27:18,080 --> 00:27:19,800
*music builds dramatically*

327
00:27:20,360 --> 00:27:21,360
Anno, come on!

328
00:27:22,520 --> 00:27:23,560
He's alive!

329
00:27:24,600 --> 00:27:26,760
Hitler! He survived!

330
00:27:26,840 --> 00:27:28,840
*he coughs violently*

331
00:27:28,920 --> 00:27:30,360
We have to go!

332
00:27:30,440 --> 00:27:32,200
*loud creaking*

333
00:27:38,400 --> 00:27:39,760
Anno!

334
00:27:50,320 --> 00:27:51,760
Over there! Come on!

335
00:27:54,520 --> 00:27:56,720
*he breathes nervously*

336
00:27:56,800 --> 00:27:57,960
(Anno) Gereon?

337
00:27:59,840 --> 00:28:01,200
*music fades*

338
00:28:01,920 --> 00:28:03,240
Gereon.

339
00:28:05,080 --> 00:28:07,640
*heavy, droning music*
*snapping fingers*

340
00:28:12,400 --> 00:28:14,480
(Anno) Shh, shh!

341
00:28:26,320 --> 00:28:27,640
*muffled bang*

342
00:28:29,440 --> 00:28:32,000
Gereon!

343
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:37,480
Breathe calmly.

344
00:28:39,920 --> 00:28:41,000
In.

345
00:28:43,160 --> 00:28:44,160
And out.

346
00:28:53,920 --> 00:28:55,680
Let go of your thoughts.

347
00:28:59,240 --> 00:29:01,000
And breathe very deeply.

348
00:29:03,440 --> 00:29:04,480
In.

349
00:29:06,160 --> 00:29:07,320
And out.

350
00:29:07,400 --> 00:29:08,920
*gentle flute music*

351
00:29:10,240 --> 00:29:11,800
I will guide you.

352
00:29:14,000 --> 00:29:15,520
Step by step.

353
00:29:17,680 --> 00:29:20,840
Step by step.

354
00:29:23,120 --> 00:29:24,200
All the way...

355
00:29:26,840 --> 00:29:28,040
to the end.

356
00:29:32,560 --> 00:29:34,720
*gently drifting piano music*

357
00:30:03,520 --> 00:30:04,680
Gereon!

358
00:30:10,400 --> 00:30:12,600
*muffled bang, crashing*

359
00:30:23,440 --> 00:30:25,640
*music swells emotionally*

360
00:30:40,320 --> 00:30:42,880
*muffled banging*

361
00:30:46,080 --> 00:30:48,480
*echoing collapse*

362
00:30:56,160 --> 00:30:57,640
*music fades*

363
00:31:01,600 --> 00:31:03,160
*faint rumbling*

364
00:31:10,320 --> 00:31:12,120
"Ladies and gentlemen,

365
00:31:12,200 --> 00:31:15,440
we apologize
for the false report.

366
00:31:15,600 --> 00:31:18,640
The Reich Chancellor
Adolf Hitler is unharmed.

367
00:31:19,360 --> 00:31:21,720
We have, however, received word

368
00:31:21,800 --> 00:31:24,520
that the Reichstag is in flames.

369
00:31:26,720 --> 00:31:30,280
Communist agitators
have set a fire,

370
00:31:30,360 --> 00:31:32,720
thereby expressing their contempt
for the German people

371
00:31:33,320 --> 00:31:36,440
in no uncertain terms.

372
00:31:36,520 --> 00:31:39,000
Our Reich Chancellor Hitler..."
- Arrest them!

373
00:31:39,080 --> 00:31:42,240
"...calls upon the German people
for the greatest possible unity.

374
00:31:42,320 --> 00:31:45,440
Quote:
'Let none of these arsonists

375
00:31:45,600 --> 00:31:48,200
or the Communists behind them
escape.

376
00:31:48,640 --> 00:31:51,520
They want to destroy our state

377
00:31:51,600 --> 00:31:53,640
and subjugate our country
to the Soviets.

378
00:31:54,520 --> 00:31:57,440
Let us hunt them down
to every last corner,

379
00:31:58,080 --> 00:31:59,320
to the ends of the earth.

380
00:32:00,320 --> 00:32:02,960
Let none of them get away.

381
00:32:03,040 --> 00:32:06,640
Tonight, they have shown us
their true face.'"

382
00:32:06,800 --> 00:32:08,960
*eerie music*

383
00:32:16,120 --> 00:32:17,600
*groaning*

384
00:32:19,560 --> 00:32:24,200
(Litten) Hans Litten, arrested
on the night of the Reichstag fire.

385
00:32:24,280 --> 00:32:26,520
As a fierce opponent of Hitler,
Hans Litten

386
00:32:26,600 --> 00:32:29,400
was tormented
in various concentration camps,

387
00:32:29,480 --> 00:32:35,760
until he escaped another interrogation
at Dachau on February 5, 1938,

388
00:32:35,840 --> 00:32:37,600
by taking his own life.

389
00:32:38,600 --> 00:32:40,400
*speaking Dutch*

390
00:32:59,800 --> 00:33:03,080
(Gennat) Ernst Gennat remained,
even under the National Socialists,

391
00:33:03,160 --> 00:33:05,040
head of Homicide Investigations

392
00:33:05,520 --> 00:33:10,360
and in 1935 was promoted to Government
and Criminal Councilor.

393
00:33:11,080 --> 00:33:14,880
He died in August 1939 in Berlin.

394
00:33:17,960 --> 00:33:19,680
(SA man laughs) Let's go.

395
00:33:20,680 --> 00:33:22,360
(Helfers) Rosa Helfers.

396
00:33:22,440 --> 00:33:25,800
In 1933, she was dismissed
from all public offices.

397
00:33:25,880 --> 00:33:29,400
Beginning in 1935,
she was surveilled by the Gestapo,

398
00:33:29,480 --> 00:33:31,520
arrested in 1944,

399
00:33:31,600 --> 00:33:34,280
and interned at the Buchholz
Labor Re-education Camp,

400
00:33:34,360 --> 00:33:37,520
where her health
was permanently damaged.

401
00:33:37,600 --> 00:33:42,440
From 1945 onward, she played
a decisive role in rebuilding the SPD.

402
00:33:42,600 --> 00:33:44,000
(Thälmann) Ernst Thälmann,

403
00:33:44,080 --> 00:33:46,440
leader of the Communist Party
of Germany,

404
00:33:46,520 --> 00:33:49,400
arrested on March 3, 1933.

405
00:33:50,000 --> 00:33:54,400
In August 1944,
after over eleven years in solitary,

406
00:33:54,480 --> 00:33:57,760
he was shot, presumably
on Adolf Hitler's direct orders.

407
00:33:58,720 --> 00:34:02,240
(Wels) Otto Wels,
party chairman of the SPD.

408
00:34:02,320 --> 00:34:05,240
Under his leadership,
the Social Democrats

409
00:34:05,320 --> 00:34:08,480
on March 23, 1933,

410
00:34:08,560 --> 00:34:12,679
as the only party, voted unanimously
against Hitler's Enabling Act,

411
00:34:12,760 --> 00:34:16,000
upon which the National Socialist
dictatorship was founded.

412
00:34:16,480 --> 00:34:18,800
Otto Wels died in exile in Paris,

413
00:34:18,880 --> 00:34:21,320
two weeks
after Hitler's invasion of Poland.

414
00:34:21,480 --> 00:34:24,040
(Heymann) Immediately
after the Reichstag fire,

415
00:34:24,120 --> 00:34:27,920
all fundamental constitutional rights
were suspended.

416
00:34:28,000 --> 00:34:30,840
Newspapers that did not conform
to the Nazi line

417
00:34:30,920 --> 00:34:34,440
were systematically banned,
journalists persecuted.

418
00:34:34,520 --> 00:34:37,639
One of the most prominent victims
was Carl von Ossietzky.

419
00:34:38,159 --> 00:34:40,199
The future Nobel Peace Prize laureate

420
00:34:40,280 --> 00:34:44,280
died after five years of detention
from the effects of mistreatment.

421
00:34:45,120 --> 00:34:47,719
(Doris) In what was no longer
a free election on March 5th,

422
00:34:47,800 --> 00:34:49,920
the NSDAP did gain ground,

423
00:34:50,000 --> 00:34:52,719
but failed to achieve
the hoped-for absolute majority.

424
00:34:53,960 --> 00:34:57,360
It was the last election
in Germany until 1948.

425
00:34:57,840 --> 00:34:59,200
*quiet scratching*

426
00:34:59,920 --> 00:35:02,520
(Blank) In early July 1934,

427
00:35:02,600 --> 00:35:05,320
Hitler had numerous
intra-party rivals murdered

428
00:35:05,400 --> 00:35:08,320
in the course of
the so-called Röhm Putsch.

429
00:35:08,480 --> 00:35:10,240
*scream, gunshot*

430
00:35:15,560 --> 00:35:17,720
(von Schleicher) Among the victims
of these events,

431
00:35:17,800 --> 00:35:21,640
also referred to by propaganda
as the Night of the Long Knives,

432
00:35:21,720 --> 00:35:24,600
were former Reich Chancellor
Kurt von Schleicher

433
00:35:24,680 --> 00:35:26,960
and his wife Elisabeth.

434
00:35:28,720 --> 00:35:30,880
*calm, foreboding music*

435
00:35:51,240 --> 00:35:54,120
(Wendt) Rudolf Diels,
founder of the Gestapo,

436
00:35:54,200 --> 00:35:57,760
handed his post over
to Heinrich Himmler in 1934

437
00:35:57,840 --> 00:36:01,040
and subsequently became
Regional President in Cologne.

438
00:36:01,800 --> 00:36:06,600
In 1957, he died
in a hunting accident.

439
00:36:11,760 --> 00:36:13,080
We'll wait outside.

440
00:36:16,160 --> 00:36:19,040
(Forster) Dr. Edmund Forster,
after his journey to Paris,

441
00:36:19,120 --> 00:36:20,800
returned to Germany.

442
00:36:22,720 --> 00:36:27,800
On August 31, 1933,
he was suspended from duty

443
00:36:28,440 --> 00:36:32,160
for alleged statements
against National Socialism.

444
00:36:33,560 --> 00:36:35,920
A few days later,
he took his own life.

445
00:36:38,040 --> 00:36:39,360
*gunshot*

446
00:36:44,000 --> 00:36:45,760
(Weiß) Ernst Weiß
used the records

447
00:36:45,840 --> 00:36:47,760
and conversations of Dr. Forster

448
00:36:47,840 --> 00:36:51,080
as the basis for his final novel,
"Der Augenzeuge" ("The Eyewitness").

449
00:36:51,840 --> 00:36:53,520
After the entry
of German troops,

450
00:36:53,600 --> 00:36:58,160
he slit his wrists
in his hotel in Paris in 1940.

451
00:37:03,160 --> 00:37:05,960
(Helga) For the business elite,
their support

452
00:37:06,040 --> 00:37:09,520
for the National Socialists
paid off handsomely.

453
00:37:10,520 --> 00:37:13,800
Not least through the massive
use of forced laborers,

454
00:37:13,880 --> 00:37:16,360
they were able
to multiply their private fortunes

455
00:37:16,440 --> 00:37:20,200
and prove themselves willing
executioners for the terror state.

456
00:37:20,640 --> 00:37:22,640
After the war, hardly any of them

457
00:37:22,720 --> 00:37:24,360
were held accountable.

458
00:37:35,360 --> 00:37:36,600
"Charlotte.

459
00:37:38,280 --> 00:37:39,760
It is unpleasant enough

460
00:37:39,840 --> 00:37:42,640
when the air above the land
turns poisonous and acrid.

461
00:37:43,640 --> 00:37:46,840
But this air can be shut out,
to a certain extent.

462
00:37:48,520 --> 00:37:50,720
One can seal one's windows tight

463
00:37:51,520 --> 00:37:52,720
and retreat into the four walls

464
00:37:52,800 --> 00:37:55,560
of a private life
carved out for oneself.

465
00:37:56,480 --> 00:38:00,520
One can withdraw,
put flowers in one's room,

466
00:38:00,600 --> 00:38:03,200
and cover one's ears
and hold one's nose on the street.

467
00:38:04,400 --> 00:38:08,360
The temptation to do so
is great, even for me.

468
00:38:12,200 --> 00:38:14,560
My brother said that in 100 years,

469
00:38:14,720 --> 00:38:17,760
people would look back
on us and our time

470
00:38:17,840 --> 00:38:19,920
and marvel
at how far we'd come,

471
00:38:21,080 --> 00:38:23,760
how many new and good things
we had tried.

472
00:38:24,880 --> 00:38:27,000
And how much
we had also failed.

473
00:38:28,040 --> 00:38:29,360
Perhaps.

474
00:38:31,760 --> 00:38:35,200
But what is now everywhere called
the Thousand-Year Reich

475
00:38:35,880 --> 00:38:37,800
will cause all of that to be forgotten.

476
00:38:40,320 --> 00:38:43,320
Until even we forget
what we once were.

477
00:38:45,840 --> 00:38:47,600
But one thing I know for certain:

478
00:38:48,720 --> 00:38:51,520
that I will never forget
what you were to me.

479
00:38:53,360 --> 00:38:56,320
So I think of you today
and tomorrow.

480
00:38:57,440 --> 00:38:59,160
And I already miss you so much,

481
00:38:59,240 --> 00:39:01,200
like nothing else in my life.

482
00:39:04,840 --> 00:39:05,920
Your laughter,

483
00:39:06,840 --> 00:39:07,920
your eyes,

484
00:39:08,480 --> 00:39:09,640
your radiance.

485
00:39:12,960 --> 00:39:15,480
Even the city has turned
gray without you.

486
00:39:19,040 --> 00:39:20,400
The city of Berlin,

487
00:39:21,200 --> 00:39:22,760
the city you showed me,

488
00:39:24,120 --> 00:39:26,320
where we were happy
for a time.

489
00:39:28,400 --> 00:39:30,400
The city that no longer exists.

490
00:39:31,600 --> 00:39:32,680
Our Berlin."

491
00:39:32,760 --> 00:39:35,520
*suspenseful,
dynamic music*

492
00:39:41,760 --> 00:39:44,600
*"Zu Asche, zu Staub"
by Severija Janušauskaitė*

493
00:39:46,040 --> 00:39:48,640
*jazz music with spoken word*

494
00:39:48,800 --> 00:39:51,200
# To ashes, to dust #

495
00:39:52,160 --> 00:39:54,120
# Stolen from the light #

496
00:39:55,800 --> 00:39:57,720
# But not just yet #

497
00:40:00,160 --> 00:40:03,240
# Miracles wait until the last #

498
00:40:10,320 --> 00:40:13,080
# Ocean of time #

499
00:40:14,360 --> 00:40:16,240
# An eternal law #

500
00:40:17,760 --> 00:40:20,240
# To ashes, to dust #

501
00:40:21,480 --> 00:40:23,200
# To ashes #

502
00:40:23,280 --> 00:40:25,400
# But not just yet #

503
00:40:30,960 --> 00:40:33,400
*lively clarinet melody*

504
00:40:52,560 --> 00:40:55,760
# To ashes, to dust #

505
00:40:56,240 --> 00:40:58,920
# Stolen from the light #

506
00:40:59,840 --> 00:41:02,720
# But not just yet #

507
00:41:04,080 --> 00:41:06,520
# Miracles wait until the last... #

508
00:41:08,000 --> 00:41:09,000
*

